   "q": "Find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ for $y = 4x^3 - \\frac{5}{x} + 2\\sqrt{x}$.",
   "expr": {
    "answer": "12x^2 + 5/x^2 + 1/sqrt(x)",
    "vars": [
     "x"
    ],
    "domain": [
     0.5,
     3
    ],
    "lhs": "\\frac{dy}{dx} =",
    "mistakes": [
     {
      "expr": "12x^2 - 5/x^2 + 1/sqrt(x)",
      "why": "$-\\frac{5}{x} = -5x^{-1}$ has derivative $-5(-1)x^{-2} = +\\frac{5}{x^2}$: two negatives make a positive."
     },
     {
      "expr": "12x^2 + 5/x^2 + 2/sqrt(x)",
      "why": "$2\\sqrt{x} = 2x^{1/2}$ has derivative $2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$; the 2 and the $\\frac{1}{2}$ cancel."
     }
    ]
   },
   "why": {
    "correct": "Rewrite: $4x^3 - 5x^{-1} + 2x^{1/2}$. Then $12x^2 + 5x^{-2} + x^{-1/2} = 12x^2 + \\frac{5}{x^2} + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$."
   }
  },
